From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rpcrt4: NDR [pipe] support in the interpreter (3310)

CORE review: RPC (DECISIONS-2 RPC-NDR).

A procedure with a pipe parameter (Oi2 HasPipes, parameter attribute
IsPipe, FC_PIPE type format) is no longer refused with
RPC_X_WRONG_STUB_VERSION.  A pipe is a sequence of chunks: a count
(4 byte aligned) and that many elements marshalled as the pipe's element
type, ended by a chunk of none; the pipes follow the other [in] parameters
of a request and come before the other [out] parameters and the return
value of a response, in the order of the parameters.

Client (ndr_client_call): the elements of the [in] pipes are pulled from the
caller's Pull routine (in pieces of at most 64 KB) after the other [in]
parameters, the elements of the [out] pipes are handed to its Push routine
(into buffers from its Alloc, in the pieces Alloc gave), with a final Push of
none.  Server (NdrStubCall2): the manager routine gets a pipe whose Pull
reads the request and whose Push writes the response; a pipe the manager
left unread is skipped, one it did not end is ended.  Whole directions are
buffered (no streaming); the wire format is the streamed one.
---

diff --git a/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_pipe.c b/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_pipe.c
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/dlls/rpcrt4/ndr_pipe.c
@@ -0,0 +1,554 @@
+/*
+ * NDR [pipe] support for the interpreter
+ *
+ * A pipe is a sequence of chunks.  A chunk is the number of elements it holds (a 32 bit
+ * integer, 4 byte aligned) followed by the elements, each marshalled as the type of the pipe's
+ * elements; the sequence ends with a chunk of no elements.  The data of the pipes follows the
+ * other parameters in the buffer: the [in] pipes follow the other [in] parameters of the
+ * request, and the [out] pipes come before the other [out] parameters and the return value of
+ * the response, in the order of the parameters.
+ *
+ * The stubs here buffer a whole direction of the call: a client collects all the elements of its
+ * [in] pipes (with their pull callbacks) before it sends the request, and the elements of the
+ * [out] pipes are pushed to the caller once the response is complete; a server hands the [in]
+ * elements of the request to its manager routine as it pulls them and collects what it pushes
+ * into the response, which is sent when the routine returns.  The wire format is the one of
+ * streamed pipes, so a peer which streams interoperates, but a pipe cannot be longer than
+ * memory and the two directions do not overlap.
+ *
+ * Copyright 2026 Stained Glass OS
+ *
